4. Deployment and the shard model

This is the most important architectural decision in the project. Read it twice.

The problem

No server is available. Multiple FLMs need to enter data. The Ops Leader needs a consolidated view. SQLite over SMB corrupts under concurrent writes.

The solution: shard by owner

Every running instance owns exactly one database file and writes only to that file.

<workspace_root>/
    config/
        taxonomy.yaml
        app.yaml
    shards/
        cw_AP_sharan.db          <- Sharan's app writes only here
        cw_R2R_priya.db          <- Priya's app writes only here
        cw_O2C_arjun.db
    evidence/
        AP_sharan/<sha256>.bin
        R2R_priya/<sha256>.bin
    exports/

Because no two writers ever touch the same file, there is no lock contention and no corruption risk.

Three workspace modes

Solo — workspace_root is a local folder. One user, zero infrastructure. Works out of the box on first run.

Team — workspace_root is a mapped shared drive. Each user's app creates and writes its own shard. This is the target deployment.

Courier — no shared drive available. Each user exports a .cwx bundle (a zip of their shard plus referenced evidence) and sends it in. The Ops Leader points leader mode at a folder of .cwx files. Same merge logic, different source.

Leader mode

Read-only aggregation. On launch in leader mode the app:

Scans shards/ for cw_*.db (or exports/ for *.cwx).
Opens each with a read-only URI: sqlite:///file:path?mode=ro&uri=true.
Checks shard_meta.schema_version; warns and skips on mismatch rather than failing.
Copies rows into an in-memory aggregate database.
Runs all dashboard and report queries against the aggregate.

Leader mode never writes to a shard. Enforce this at the session factory level, not by convention.

Always display a freshness strip: owner name, tower, last_write_at, and a stale flag if older than 7 days. A leader must never mistake "nobody updated their shard" for "there were no incidents."

ID collision

Case display IDs must embed the owner code or shards will collide on merge.

Format: {TOWER}-{OWNER_CODE}-{YYYY}-{NNNN} → AP-SM-2026-0042

Primary keys are UUIDv4. The display ID is a separate human-facing column.

5. Severity tiering

The tier is what keeps the tool from becoming a burden. Tier is assigned at intake and determines what the app demands from the user.

Tier	Trigger	Required	Clock
A	Client-visible, OR value at risk ≥ threshold, OR SLA breached	Full RCA (5 Whys + is/is-not) + corrective and preventive action + effectiveness verification	RCA within 48h
B	Internally caught and contained, no client impact	5 Whys + corrective action	5 working days
C	Near-miss or observation	Nothing. Log and auto-close.	None

Thresholds live in taxonomy.yaml. The app proposes a tier from the intake answers and lets the user override upward, never silently downward.

Tier C matters. Near-miss volume is the leading indicator and it is what makes the Pareto chart meaningful. Make logging one genuinely trivial.

