                old_value, new_value, actor, at

status on case: draft → investigating → capa_open → verifying → closed. Tier C skips straight to closed.

Evidence storage. Files are content-addressed by SHA-256 and deduplicated — the same attachment across ten cases stores once. visibility controls whether a file appears in a client-facing report; default to internal and require an explicit opt-in to promote. Enforce a per-file size cap from config.

Audit log is append-only. No update or delete paths. Ever.

7. Screens
Quick log (agent)

Modal. Maximum six fields: tower, sub-process, what happened, when, client-visible (yes/no), value at risk (optional). The app infers the tier and tells the user what happens next. Submit and close.

Duplicate detection at intake

When a case is submitted, immediately surface up to three similar past cases — matched on sub-process plus cause category plus a keyword match on title, within the last 12 months. Show them before the RCA is started, not in a quarterly review. This prevents duplicate RCAs and puts "we have seen this before" in front of the person who can act on it.

My desk (FLM)

The default landing screen. Four stacked sections, most urgent first: overdue items, due this week, RCAs pending, actions awaiting effectiveness verification. If anything is overdue, it renders before anything else on launch.

Case detail

Tabs: Summary, RCA, Actions, Evidence, History. The RCA tab shows 5 Whys as a simple sequential form. The is/is-not framing and fishbone appear only for Tier A, and the fishbone is optional even then — most users will not complete it honestly, and a half-filled fishbone is worse than none.

Dashboard (leader)

Filters across the top: date range, tower, severity, owner. Charts below (see §8).

Reports

Template picker, date range, output format, redaction toggle. Writes to exports/ and offers to open the folder.

Settings

Workspace path, owner identity, mode (solo/team/courier/leader), taxonomy file path, backup and restore.

8. Dashboard metrics

These specific metrics, in this language. They are what an ops leader presents.

Client-detected vs internally caught, as a ratio, trended monthly. The single most persuasive number in a client governance meeting.
Repeat rate — % of this month's cases whose primary cause category also appeared in the previous six months. Flag repeats explicitly on the case list.
CAPA closure rate and aging, split by owner.
Defects per tower, normalised per 1,000 transactions where volume data exists in config; absolute counts otherwise, clearly labelled as such.
Value at risk in currency, Tier A only.
Effectiveness verification pass rate — of verified actions, how many held up at the 30/60-day check.
Pareto of primary cause categories.
Initiative RAG rollup.
