10. Build order

Vertical slices. Each slice ends with something demoable. Do not build all models, then all routes.

S1 — Skeleton. pywebview shell, first-run wizard, shard creation, quick log, case list. Package this as an EXE before starting S2. Packaging failures found in week eight are expensive; found in week one they are trivial.
S2 — Case depth. Case detail, 5 Whys, actions with owners and due dates, My desk.
S3 — Evidence. Drag-drop, hashing, dedupe, visibility flags.
S4 — Dashboard. ECharts, filters, repeat detection, duplicate-at-intake.
S5 — Reports. PPTX monthly pack first, then PDF single case, then HTML snapshot.
S6 — Leader mode. Shard scan, read-only merge, freshness strip, .cwx courier import/export.
S7 — Initiatives. Deliberately last and deliberately thin.
11. Conventions
All paths resolve relative to workspace_root. No absolute paths anywhere in code.
Config is read once at startup into a typed object; no scattered yaml.load calls.
Every route that mutates state writes an audit row in the same transaction.
Seed script generates 200 synthetic cases across three towers and twelve months so the dashboard has something to show on a fresh install.
Git from the first commit. Never commit *.db, evidence/, or exports/.
Type hints on all function signatures. ruff for linting.
12. Known traps
PyInstaller and pywebview. Hidden imports and data files for the frontend bundle need explicit --add-data entries. Test the frozen build early and often.
Antivirus. An unsigned PyInstaller binary will be quarantined by corporate AV and SmartScreen. This is an IT whitelisting conversation that takes longer than the development — it should already be in flight.
SQLite over SMB. Read-only is fine. Concurrent writes are not. The shard model exists specifically to avoid this; do not shortcut it.
Stale shards. A leader looking at a dashboard where half the team has not saved in three weeks will draw wrong conclusions. The freshness strip is not optional.
Fishbone theatre. If users are filling the 6M categories with filler to get past the screen, remove it rather than defending it.
